ZyperOS: the vertex decoder JIT decodes two vertices a loop, from
Deadstation Portable (github.com/tyrannotorus/emulator-deadstation-portable, GPL-2.0-or-later as PPSSPP),
a PPSSPP v1.20.4 fork for the TrimUI Brick: its commits e730ed2, 4540404, d4cb377, 063b389,
4d55b76, 5979a57, 09f3297 (the pair loops) and af5d1b2 (a skinned vertex's normal and position
loaded first and transformed together).

The one-vertex loop is one chain of dependent loads, conversions and multiplies, and an in-order
core (the Brick's Cortex-A53, the Pixel 2's Cortex-A35) waits on each. The pair loop puts two
vertices' chains side by side, an instruction of each in turn, with the same loads, the same
arithmetic in the same order for each vertex, and the same stores: the same bytes out. An odd last
vertex runs the one-vertex code once. Unskinned vertices of 24 bytes or more are prefetched 256
bytes ahead. Formats the pair code does not cover keep the old loop, and upstream's two hand-written
C++ decoders (VertexDecoderHandwritten.cpp) are still chosen first for their formats.

Kept from upstream that the fork had cut: the ARM64 guard and the decoder log.

